        {
          "id": "the_temperature_adjustment_is_all_humidity_at_this_hour",
          "description": "S15's temperature arm, adjust.rs:87-141. `temperatureadjustment` carries four rows and every one is polProcessID 301 on regClassID 42/46/47/48 for model years 2027-2060, so neither the exact-class lookup nor the regClassID 0 wildcard finds anything and every A and B term is 0 -- the temperature half of the factor is exactly 1 on all six pollutant-processes. THE HUMIDITY HALF IS NOT. NOx alone takes k, and k is 1.0216 on gasoline and E85 (CFR 86, reading specific humidity below its 10.71 reference) and 1.00955 on diesel (CFR 1065, reading the water mole fraction); electricity has no `noxhumidityadjust` row and takes 1. Dropping k would move every NOx row by over 2 %, which no per-pollutant sum tolerance in tolerance.toml would catch. The A/C arm is inert, and inert by a CLAMP: the activity term is -0.297 before setup.rs:411 clamps it to [0, 1] -- 7.934 degF of heat index below the 67.4340633418 at which it would cross 0.\n\nk IS NOW COMPUTED FROM THE WEATHER, NOT READ. The two k values quoted above are hand-checkable from what `ZoneMonthHour` stores -- `1 - 0.0329 x (10.053684293477 - 10.71)` and `1 / (9.953 x 0.015929059029 + 0.832)` -- and are kept that way because that is the arithmetic a reader can redo. Since docs/meteorology-generator.md 8.2 this document derives the humidity instead of reading it and lands 1.02159279434712 and 1.009548388483679, 7.4418e-09 and 3.6203e-09 away. The five assertions on those two values carry `rel: 1e-7`, and what they test is now the whole chain from `temperature`, `relHumidity` and `County.barometricPressure` down to k, against MOVES's own stored answer.",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0.0,
            "end": 0.0
          },
